Lesson lines for words Apertium's bilingual dictionary does not carry, or
carries with a meaning the translator cannot use.

WHY THEY ARE NOT IN ../italian.txt AND ../spanish.txt, which is the whole
point of this directory: those two files are the TAGGER's corpus. The
generator draws its lesson shapes from them, so a line added there changes
the training pairs, and `generated/' and `model.rows' must be regenerated
with it -- which re-rolls every minority shape the network learned by luck.
Four lines cost the adjective grid 0.60 -> 0.25 and a red test/tagger.pl.

The vocabulary directory is NOT read by the generator. These lines are
written at the HEAD of `../vocabulary/<language>.txt' by `../build.pl',
before every dictionary line, so the translator gets the words, the first
meaning of a word is the one written here, and the tagger sees nothing at
all.

One sentence a line, the same controlled English the reader already reads.

eng-ita.dix and eng-spa.dix are the other half: ENTRIES in the bilingual
dictionary's own shape, one a line, for a word the monolingual dictionary
has with its paradigm and the bilingual one lacks -- `muovere', `sparire',
`soccorritore' have every form in apertium-ita.ita.dix and no English. The
builder reads them before the raw bilingual file, so every form comes out
of the paradigm exactly as a dictionary word's does, where a line in
italian.txt would have to state each form by hand. An entry marked r="RL"
is read into English only and comes after every entry both ways; an entry
both ways is the first meaning of its word and the first word for its
meaning. Each file says which of its entries is which, and why.
